#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:5
#: d52e20241f694c0d82c2e01915339d2f
msgid "Terrain Following"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:7
#: 22be302b26244f4393634c1e33de63d0
msgid "You can use automatic terrain following for fixed wing aircraft. This page explains how terrain following works, how to enable it and what its limitations are."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:11
#: b3ef3b0956f34978b257b5bb2e25b1de
msgid "If using Copter, see :ref:`Copter specific terrain following instructions here <copter:terrain-following>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:17
#: eb05da0ae2ef4ec5a4bca06c750da72f
msgid "How it works"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:19
#: 963638df945c4e7db454f8cde758092b
msgid "Terrain following works by maintaining a terrain database on the autopilot which gives the terrain height in meters above sea level for a grid of geographic locations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:22
#: 3e86ae83856a40998d94d620ad021c78
msgid "If the autopilot has a microSD card this database is stored in the APM/TERRAIN directory on the microSD card."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:23
#: f6cb2d5a69d1444ea3b3114c80359d83
msgid "If the autopilot does not have a microSD card, there is an option to enable data to be retrieved directly from the GCS into RAM on the autopilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:24
#: 1c44b3725cfe49549a0cc776dac8694a
msgid "On SITL the database is stored in the ./terrain folder relative to the directory where the SITL process is running"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:26
#: 3da1faeae8744c2b85ae0f9ae47fc239
msgid "The database is populated automatically by the autopilot requesting terrain data from the ground station over a MAVLink telemetry link. This can happen either during flight planning when the autopilot is connected over USB, or during flight when connected over a radio link."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:31
#: 64d02045fb024e7abb6687cdcec9956c
msgid "One can directly download terrain data via PC to the SD card using the this `web utility <https://terrain.ardupilot.org/>`__. See below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:33
#: 0b645d9a13c24afb8dc212f7c7c4ea74
msgid "The data is only transferred if the autopilot has a GPS lock. So to make sure your missions have the terrain data prior to flight (in case the ground station is not connected during the flight and/or does not have internet connection to obtain the data), be sure that GPS lock is in effect when loading the mission to the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:36
#: 4e4238e71ebe48fca78be45e7f940ce4
msgid "SD Card support for Terrain data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:38
#: 0a48a5c6d1594685a48eef3219d222f5
msgid "Terrain following works best if the autopilot has an onboard SD card for storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:40
#: 72890414ea4844e4a3566bea93629d4e
msgid "Once the terrain data is sent from the GCS to the autopilot it is stored on the microSD card, so that it is available even when the GCS is not connected. This makes it possible for an autopilot with an SD card to use terrain data to perform a terrain following RTL (Return To Launch) even when it is not able to talk to the ground station."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:46
#: de8d542c3b6144af9d45d31a28b529ce
msgid "During flight the ArduPilot code automatically pages in the needed terrain data from the microSD card into memory as the aircraft approaches a new area. By default it maintains an area of about 7km by 8km in memory if the default terrain grid spacing (100m) is used. See below for options to change the default behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:52
#: ca97b6d502584d988c9c205653cfdf36
msgid "In addition to any terrain data for the immediate vicinity of the aircraft, ArduPilot also asks the ground station for terrain data for any mission waypoints which are loaded, and for any rally points which are loaded. This ensures that terrain data is available on the microSD card for a whole mission even if the GCS becomes unavailable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:59
#: cbadbabbfd92414ca48459d5f1d31626
msgid "Terrain Following Flight Modes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:61
#: 18168e225c724778b03b403389ac6df9
msgid "Use of terrain following in specific flight modes is controlled by the :ref:`TERRAIN_FOLLOW<TERRAIN_FOLLOW>` parameter. That parameter defaults to off, so no terrain following will be used in those modes. Setting the bitmask in :ref:`TERRAIN_FOLLOW<TERRAIN_FOLLOW>` determines which altitude controlled modes terrain following is active. For example, setting it to \"10\" enables following in FBWB and AUTO. Setting it to \"1\" enables it in all available modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:66
#: 3e1e21c7b281456eb211459210ba73e0
msgid "Terrain Following in non auto modes can be disabled with an RC switch assigned ``RCx_OPTION`` = 86. When enabling (<1200us) or disabling (>1800us) terrain following with the switch, the present altitude will be used as the target set point either above terrain, or home, respectively. The target altitude can be changed as normal with elevator whether the altitude reference being used is above home or above terrain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:72
#: 9fb81a6f39a14aa28796e33d88eff0cb
msgid "Use of terrain following in AUTO missions is controlled on a waypoint by waypoint basis using the reference frame of the waypoint. Normal (non terrain following) waypoints have a \"Relative\" reference frame, and altitudes are specified relative to the home location, not by the :ref:`TERRAIN_FOLLOW<TERRAIN_FOLLOW>` parameter. Terrain following waypoints have a \"Terrain\" reference frame, and altitudes are relative to the ground level given in the terrain database."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:79
#: f39ed3cf6e9b43719ae81c67241a88a5
msgid "in AUTO mode autolandings, the :ref:`TERRAIN_FOLLOW<TERRAIN_FOLLOW>` parameter does have an impact, when a rangefinder is also used. See :ref:`rangefinder-autolanding`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:82
#: f98cf100046a45eab6c86a79ff3c837c
msgid "See :ref:`common-understanding-altitude` for altitude definitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:86
#: eb42830696494efeb2f0fb1d2149baf7
msgid "Uses of Terrain Following"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:88
#: cc58e900dd054cb7bd8913f2250241b1
msgid "Terrain following is very useful when flying ArduPilot in areas where the terrain may vary significantly. Key uses are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:91
#: aadcbb3b1c69495e81c352a6777edc6b
msgid "**Safe RTL**. Being able to come over a hill rather than trying to fly through it when you enter RTL in a hilly area is very useful!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:93
#: ef89301c9e0e412bae4ed118453d9f24
msgid "**Aerial Photography.** It is useful to be able to maintain a constant altitude over the ground when taking a sequence of aerial photos"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:96
#: d0e8a9f63f1343cd80371844fff41694
msgid "**FPV flying.** When flying FPV in CRUISE mode it is useful to maintain constant height above the ground so you can spend more time enjoying the scenary and less time avoiding hills"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:99
#: f3c314cbcca143ea82f18acb6b8b002c
msgid "**Regulatory Compliance.** Many countries have restrictions on the altitude drones must fly at or below, often 400' (about 120m). With terrain following enabled, missions items and other limits can be specified in AGL (terrain) frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:105
#: a3bc85dd46884ba7b54be72f926e9b78
msgid "Sources of Terrain Data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:107
#: c77ea957fe3145f4a13526bbb1935cf9
msgid "The ground station is normally responsible for providing the raw terrain data which is sent to the aircraft via MAVLink. Mission Planner, QGroundControl, SiYi UniGCS and MAVProxy support the required TERRAIN_DATA and TERRAIN_REQUEST MAVLink messages needed for terrain following download support."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:111
#: 7eff08daed9047bea122f0df88a0f88c
msgid "Mission Planner and MAVProxy supply the ArduPilot JAXA ALOS sourced data for 100m and 30m spacing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:112
#: 7928366df4954ec3868682b0b478e6c3
msgid "QGroundControl supplies COP30 (Copernicus DEM - Global and European Digital Elevation Model) sourced data for 100m and 30m spacing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:113
#: c3a89cde4feb46ebba46c2c644104b9d
msgid "SiYi UniGCS supplies terrain data (NASA - SRTM Shuttle Radar Topography Mission Global 1 arc second V003) for 100m and 30m spacing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:116
#: 7bbaa84df1804a9aabc7365580b94e04
msgid "SiYi terrain data: NASA JPL. (2013). *NASA Shuttle Radar Topography Mission Global 1 arc second V003* \\[Data set\\]. `NASA Earth Sciences Data Sets <https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/data/catalog/lpcloud-srtmgl1-003>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:118
#: 1eba52b692264ca7b9a9fca0a9e37775
msgid "If you are using a different ground station that doesn't support TERRAIN_REQUEST and TERRAIN_DATA, in order to download terrain data you will need to to either pre-load the data on your SD card or connect using one of the above stations in order to allow ArduPilot to load terrain data onto your board on the ground or in flight. Once terrain data is loaded, it is saved permanently on the microSD card."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:123
#: f8d686ed9389490482acb1bbc5b21f48
msgid "Unless the ground control station uses a different server with support for different spacing, setting the :ref:`TERRAIN_SPACING <TERRAIN_SPACING>` parameter other than 100m or 30m provides no better resolution, and only consumes more space on the SD card."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:126
#: 461cfd0b6d3f42cfa3fecfe4204bda12
msgid "Terrain Data is downloaded any time you save or connect with a loaded mission with these ground stations or, if flying, the autopilot will request data if it's flying into an area not already downloaded, assuming the ground station can provide it. The ground station may have it's own cache of previously requested data, but if if flying in an area where there is no cached data, an internet connection is required by the ground station for this to work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:131
#: 33857adc644b436fb30b232ee467398a
msgid "If :ref:`TERRAIN_OPTIONS<TERRAIN_OPTIONS>` bit 0 (value +1) is set, the terrain data will not be downloaded from the ground station."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:133
#: 60f73fbd3161455b832aa0c329edb4bc
msgid "While the autopilot will request the terrain data around waypoints and home location, if it is not connected to a GCS that can supply enroute tiles, and if the relevant terrain data is not on the SD Card, the autopilot just interpolates between the terrain elevation of the waypoints. See below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:136
#: ef6c25d3bee246f18489d545a402bf3a
msgid "If you are not flying with a GCS that can fetch and supply terrain data as the vehicles flies between waypoints, then you may need to load terrain data covering the flight route and/or area for RTL paths manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:139
#: 4ec7aa3d77be4264aae71ee366d97842
msgid "You can download a set of terrain data tiles at either 100m (SRTM3) or 30m (SRTM1) for any anticipated flight area using this `web utility <https://terrain.ardupilot.org/>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:144
#: e71c5395e3a2416a85f9ee942aa75c39
msgid "It will create tiles for the specified radius around a geographic location. Then you can download them, unzip and write in the APM/TERRAIN folder of the SD card."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:146
#: 83e831bbbdff47b4bb02b5c5e9d5c0cd
msgid "You can also download .zip files for entire continents, or individual tiles from `here <https://terrain.ardupilot.org/continentsdat3/>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:148
#: 962968343e294746986955c409c53c04
msgid "You may also want to obtain higher resolution data, available from commercial sources online, if you have specialized requirements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:150
#: 7b6907f9edad4e5d953c40979263f186
msgid "It's a very good idea to remove the SD card from the AutoPilot and copy large terrain data sets directly to the /APM/TERRAIN folder from your PC, as copying them via MAVFtp in Mission Planner using a USB or other MAVlink connection is likely to take many, many hours or even days."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:155
#: 3297c2b0d1c845e6b882b3b90a3f60fc
msgid "Terrain Spacing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:157
#: f863d14b05b0420eb18e25434e71f0cd
msgid "The ArduPilot terrain code has a user settable parameter called :ref:`TERRAIN_SPACING<TERRAIN_SPACING>` which controls the grid spacing which is used for requests for terrain data from the aircraft to the ground station. The default :ref:`TERRAIN_SPACING<TERRAIN_SPACING>` is 100 meters but 30m spacing (SRTM1) data provides a good compromise between accuracy and bandwidth/storage space, and runtime memory requirements. Use ``TERRAIN_SPACING`` = 30 meters for most normal use cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:164
#: efc312d12014470aa6cb9133a3344737
msgid "Expert users who provide their own terrain data may set a different grid spacing for specialist applications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:166
#: f780de0523af47aaae43d2cd798b04ef
msgid "Note that the amount of terrain data kept in memory is directly related to the grid spacing. If you decrease the ``TERRAIN_SPACING`` by a factor of 2 then the amount of terrain area kept in memory is reduced by a factor of 4. This may be an issue on older boards with less memory, but for a newer AutoPilot with 1M of RAM, the :ref:`TERRAIN_CACHE_SZ<TERRAIN_CACHE_SZ>` can be increased to make better use of available memory for caching terrain tiles. The default is 12 but a good value on an STM32-H7 processor would be 60 or higher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:174
#: 7538a99c4b674eb79b69874582707eb2
msgid "If your AutoPilot has less memory, less storage space, or communication bandwidth might be an issue, it is recommended that you use a ``TERRAIN_SPACING`` of 100 meters to prevent the aircraft running off the side of a grid in flight and not having data available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:179
#: b043d47160a84e798d129c7b44112ba8
msgid "If the ground station does not have terrain data available at the resolution requested by the aircraft then the ground station will interpolate as necessary to provide the requested grid size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:184
#: c2fbe26b20d140a9abd24623776fd1d0
msgid "Terrain Data Issue - Old Terrain Data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:185
#: 9073d116aaaa41ce9f3cd23e6a2b04a8
msgid "In early 2026 an issue was identified with terrain data available from terrain.ardupilot.org. The issue was resolved on 24th February 2026. From ArduPilot 4.7, ArduPilot will raise a pre-arm error if it finds older data on the SD Card, or it's served from a ground control station, perhaps from an older cache. It is risky to fly with this old data, but the risks do depend on where you are flying, so you might choose to accept the risk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:191
#: b156f7aad5d540fb9b8470aaa53b17c9
msgid "If you are running a 4.6.x release or earlier you won’t get the above error message, but that doesn’t mean you are safe. You should:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:194
#: 5767848e9a3043ca8829b8ad3487c651
msgid "Delete all the DAT files in the APM/TERRAIN directory on your microSD card then reboot the autopilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:195
#: c226d19dc014455aad96aea1b44f186d
msgid "If using Mission Planner or MAVProxy, delete the SRTM data from your Ground Station. If using Mission Planner delete the contents of C:\\\\ProgramData\\\\Mission Planner\\\\srtm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:196
#: 925d45c3052948b8a63d7891a544548f
msgid "Then either download new data from terrain.ardupilot.org or let the normal automatic terrain download system download new terrain data via your GCS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:198
#: e3d9fb500e9244f5a031978f3ce32b2b
msgid "If you are running the 4.7.x release or higher (or you are running the latest master branch) then you should get the above warning if you have old terrain data, but if you are uncertain then the safe thing to do is delete the old DAT files from APM/TERRAIN and download again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:200
#: f7bc24c356cf47d38a3c8ab8b814b56d
msgid "In 4.7.x or higher you can set :ref:`TERRAIN_OPTIONS<TERRAIN_OPTIONS>` bit 2 (value=4) to override the check and allow you to use old terrain data. If you are confident you are not impacted by the bugs (most locations in the world are unaffected) then this new :ref:`TERRAIN_OPTIONS<TERRAIN_OPTIONS>` bit allows you to fly without the error messages.  We don’t recommend this, but for some vehicles where replacing the terrain data files is difficult this is provided as an option. For example, if you have created your own DAT files from geoTIFF or similar then this would be a way to avoid having to regenerate them. Remember to unset this option bit when you move to newer terrain data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:206
#: 33c841ba5ea647998edcb88c34e37ca8
msgid "Terrain Accuracy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:207
#: 1de3e6a60919476b8fbdd969296146c9
msgid "When the terrain data issue was resolved, the data sources for both 100m and 30m terrain were switched to JAXA's ALOS. There are 2 dataset versions available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:209
#: 69985a00178d4e588589dbc85de1aa7a
msgid "SRTM1: Based on JAXA's ALOS dataset and has 30m (1 arc-second) horizontal resolution. It covers all areas between 0 and 84 degrees North/South latitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:210
#: 493bd58a19a34853825f4f29e5a8d5a8
msgid "SRTM3: Downsampled version of SRTM1 with 100m (3 arc-second) horizontal resolution. It covers all areas between 0 and 84 degrees North/South latitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:212
#: daacd57d72e94c23a3ffd18e19142377
msgid "This does mean that although we use \"SRTM1\" and \"SRTM3\", to refer to the different data sets, neither of the data sets provided by ArduPilot is actually NASA SRTM (Shuttle Radar Topography Mission Global) data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:215
#: 4e19cf03ecf0404881ce6d9b72e40d94
msgid "While the original NASA SRTM data had a target vertical accuracy of +/- 16 meters (see `SRTM Mission Specifications <https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/data/catalog/lpcloud-srtmgl1n-003>`_), the ALOS AW3D30 data generally achieves an accuracy of +/- 5 meters (see `JAXA AW3D30 Validation Report <https://isprs-archives.copernicus.org/articles/XLI-B4/157/2016/isprs-archives-XLI-B4-157-2016.pdf>`_)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:222
#: afc14903cd8e460dba9f84ce5f31e311
msgid "Impacts of Downsampling to SRTM3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:224
#: 2f601f52589b471c8e96bb147fea68bf
msgid "While both datasets are derived from the high-accuracy JAXA ALOS mission, the process of downsampling from the native 30m resolution (SRTM1) to the 100m resolution (SRTM3) introduces several issues:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:226
#: 7ab1122483c04f099815a5fc0b42eaa1
msgid "**Vertical Accuracy Loss:** Downsampling involves averaging multiple 30m data points into a single 100m cell. This mathematical smoothing increases the vertical error (RMSE), particularly in rugged or mountainous terrain, making the 100m dataset less precise than the 30m version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:227
#: 0b18bc95523f46d897e7b2e521a5bb7f
msgid "**Loss of Sharp Features:** High-resolution details of valleys, ridges, and hills become \"flattened\" or smoothed out. The 30m (SRTM1) version is significantly more reliable at capturing sharp topographic changes that a drone might encounter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:228
#: ea68c4b9cfd14974ad3f3060af506fb9
msgid "**Averaging Errors:** Each ~100m cell in the SRTM3 version represents the average height of an area roughly the size of a city block. Small but critical obstacles—such as a single sharp peak—may be averaged into the surrounding lower terrain, making them effectively \"invisible\" to the autopilot's terrain-following logic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:231
#: cd4f27f7bca3439ab3b4f468ec677134
msgid "Regardless of which dataset is used, the terrain database represents a \"surface model\" (including trees/buildings) and can change over time. Always maintain a conservative safety altitude buffer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:233
#: 15d2014e6e1f4555a0f5a8013fa87fe9
msgid "Using terrain data for low flights is not recommended, and if using automatic landing, a rangefinder is recommended for the final approach."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:236
#: 56361ab0ca2c4965a04af0f93eeaf9b7
msgid "Setting up for terrain following using Mission Planner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:238
#: 5faa0b349d4a4b3c8bc3ff9e64e08656
msgid "To setup your fixed wing aircraft for terrain following if you are using Mission Planner, follow these steps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:240
#: ce429d816a3c44a29409852ce5d1630c
msgid "make sure you have the latest MissionPlanner installed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:241
#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:279
#: 670ca8256aae4489bd768060d90661e8
#: d90ee78258404af589a13444f271890c
msgid "set :ref:`TERRAIN_ENABLE<TERRAIN_ENABLE>` to 1 and :ref:`TERRAIN_FOLLOW<TERRAIN_FOLLOW>` to 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:242
#: 41f43b59d2304578b2132edbd21db392
msgid "connect to your vehicle over USB when you have GPS lock"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:243
#: 69e7e5724c534627a1f5299a0eedbe09
msgid "check the FlightData->Status page in MissionPlanner and look for the terrain status data:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:249
#: 60d37470706946e79cede93436b49620
msgid "When the autopilot has finished loading terrain data you should see \"ter_pend\" goes to zero and the current terrain altitude in meters showing up in \"ter_alt\". The \"ter_pend\" value is the number of terrain blocks that the autopilot is waiting to load from the ground station or from the SD Card."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:255
#: 718c428676e84964974be26f28efddfe
msgid "Terrain Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:256
#: 13283904d8534238833a14e4caad77cf
msgid "There are three optional behaviors which can be selected via the :ref:`TERRAIN_OPTIONS<TERRAIN_OPTIONS>` bitmask parameter:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:258
#: ed011b679c3a4a01addcdbc6848ff3db
msgid "if bit 0 is set (value+1): downloading of terrain data is halted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:259
#: a2d775c8306e40da8ebea8b504efdd63
msgid "if bit 1 is set (value+2): enables using terrain data (via GCS) when no SD card is present."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:260
#: bca69fd8f7a84754b73042be5c8baf3e
msgid "if bit 2 is set (value+4): will accept \"old\" terrain data (see below)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:263
#: 78830cd60f9f4051b5c53195f24206b2
msgid "Terrain Look-ahead"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:265
#: 1401e30175a240ebba08b28f76de48cc
msgid "The terrain following code \"looks ahead\" of the current position along the flight path to try to ensure that the aircraft climbs soon enough to avoid upcoming terrain. The amount of look-ahead is controlled by the :ref:`TERRAIN_LOOKAHD<TERRAIN_LOOKAHD>` parameter, which defaults to 2000 meters. The look-ahead is also limited by the distance to the next waypoint in AUTO mode, so you need to ensure that you don't have any legs of your mission which include climb rates your aircraft cannot achieve."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:273
#: 4db9ad22be9046ab9ccfc4c25e09ec76
msgid "The climb rate used in the terrain look-ahead is based on the"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:274
#: a93cbac898d74c45acf9dd54afbbb55c
msgid ":ref:`TECS_CLMB_MAX<TECS_CLMB_MAX>` parameter, combined with your current ground speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:277
#: 4c90ec3e69fa41c7848b3b5c8a30de7f
msgid "Recommended Settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:280
#: 65d272ae67cc4205bc34908cd515377b
msgid "set :ref:`TERRAIN_SPACING<TERRAIN_SPACING>` to 30 meters for more precise AGL calculations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:281
#: d3ae349b27834a09a7aa38b231f1b065
msgid "set :ref:`TERRAIN_CACHE_SZ<TERRAIN_CACHE_SZ>` to 60 to load more tiles into memory (RAM)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:282
#: 0da6d661fa4942c290be80f972e3ba9c
msgid "set :ref:`TERRAIN_LOOKAHD<TERRAIN_LOOKAHD>` to 1000 meters unless you have a reason to need more"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-terrain-following.rst:284
#: 13fbd0d7bf264e79a1cc890b1d8eac42
msgid "To avoid high bandwidth consumption due to terrain tiles being sent to the aircraft over your telemetry link, especially if you often fly in different locations, it's also highly recommended to download the terrain around your planned flight area in the /APM/TERRAIN folder on the SD Card on the AutoPilot."
msgstr ""
